{-
   A concrete position in an otherwise generic signature.

   There is no type variable at such a position, so what decides whether an argument fits is whether
   it *converts* - and that has to be the same question a non-generic signature asks, or a signature
   means two different things depending on whether some other parameter mentions a variable. The
   pairs below are that property written out: each generic function has a non-generic twin taking the
   same concrete parameter, and every call is made against both.

   The rule was stated twice until the two were made one operation (`convertibleType`), and the
   generic half was the shorter: it asked only for a `Widen` instance, so everything that converts
   without one was rejected here and accepted there. `genericWidth` is the case a well-formed program
   reaches - there is no `Widen(Id, U64)` instance and there is not meant to be one, because widening
   a refinement is structural rather than a conversion anybody declared - and it fails against the
   reverted rule with "argument 2 of genericWidth is @bits(53) U64, which does not fit U64", from a
   signature whose non-generic twin one line below accepts it.

   The other two are the halves of the rule that already held, kept so this asserts the whole of it
   rather than the part that moved: a `&` parameter, whose argument is the caller's storage in both
   signatures, and a declared widening, which is what the old rule could see.
-}
alias Id = @bits(53) U64

-- A refinement and what it refines are one type to everything that decides a type, so an `Id`
-- reaching a `U64` parameter is free in both signatures. There is no `Widen(Id, U64)` instance and
-- there is not meant to be one: widening a refinement is structural.
fn genericWidth(value: a, width: U64) -> U64 = width

fn plainWidth(value: Int, width: U64) -> U64 = width

-- A `&` parameter of a concrete type. The argument is the caller's storage either way, and both
-- signatures have to reach that conclusion before either commits to the callee.
fn genericBump(value: a, &counter: Int) -> {}:
    counter = counter + 1

fn plainBump(value: Int, &counter: Int) -> {}:
    counter = counter + 1

-- A narrower argument at a concrete position, which *is* a declared conversion - the one shape the
-- old rule could see.
fn genericWiden(value: a, total: Long) -> Long = total

fn main() -> Int:
    let narrow = 21 :: Id
    let &counter = 0 :: Int
    let small = 7 :: Int

    genericBump(1, counter)
    plainBump(1, counter)

    let widths = (genericWidth(1, narrow) :: Int) + (plainWidth(1, narrow) :: Int)

    return widths + counter + (genericWiden(1, small) :: Int)
